{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>גַּם עַתָּה כְדִבְרֵיכֶם כֶּן הוּא</b> — This too, by strict law, is true: כדבריכם כן הוא (according to your words, so it is), for all of you are liable in the matter — when theft is found in the hand of one of ten, all of them are seized. But I will act for you beyond the line of the law (לפנים מן השורה): the one with whom it is found, he alone shall be my slave. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 44:10). But the expression גם עתה (even now) does not fit well upon this interpretation. Perhaps one may say: גם עתה — even now, כדבריכם כן הוא — the law is according to your words, that the one with whom it is found shall be my slave alone. But Joseph said, חלילה לי מעשות זאת (far be it from me to do this), which indicates that their law is not that all of them should be seized for the theft. Therefore he said חלילה לי, for I am the judge of all the earth, and far be it from me to do violence to you, for there is no liability upon the ten when theft is found in the hand of one of them unless they conspired together. For when they band together to go steal and one of them takes the stolen item with the knowledge of all, then they are all liable.\n\nBut the correct interpretation is that at first he was accusing all of them: למה שלמתם רעה תחת טובה (why have you repaid evil for good?), הרעותם אשר עשיתם (the evil you have done). And they exonerated themselves by saying אשר ימצא אתו ומת (the one with whom it is found shall die), for he is the thief, and even we who are innocent of the theft shall become slaves. Their intent was clearly to say that the thief with whom it is found is the only one who knew about the theft — for if all of them were in on the theft, why should this one die while they live? It would be fitting that all of them die or all of them become slaves, for the law over them is equal. But they claim that the others had no knowledge of the theft; they merely penalized themselves that they too would become slaves. Therefore he said to them: גם עתה — even now, that you are banded together and found together, כדבריכם כן הוא — according to your words, so it is: the one with whom it is found, he is the thief, and he alone is the one deserving punishment, ויהי לי עבד (and he shall be my slave), for in his ransom I am more interested than in his death. ואתם תהיו נקיים (and you shall be innocent), for perhaps you did not know about the theft, as you said.\n\nAlternatively, the meaning of כן הוא is כן יהיה (so shall it be): גם עתה — even now, that the law is not in accordance with your words, כדבריכם כן הוא — according to your words, so shall it be. This is like ותאמר כדבריכם כן הוא ותשלחם וילכו (Joshua 2:21), whose meaning is כן יהיה (so shall it be). And this is correct in my eyes, and it is the words of our Sages in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 92:8), who said: ten men in whom theft is found — are all of them in the pot? I do not do so, etc. They intended what I have explained, not the words of Rashi.\n\nWe may also set it right according to the opinion of the Rav [Maimonides], who said that Joseph said חלילה לי מעשות זאת — far be it from me to do this, that I should be to you an evil man more than upon my house, for I released you from the start and said to you ואתם תהיו נקיים (and you shall be innocent), for I will uphold the word of my servant and complete the counsel of my messenger."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:50.018241775Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}